## helper tp fix repo in case of worktree broken

The problem is a Git worktree limitation, not a code bug in the repo:

- All worktrees share one repository and one set of branch refs. A branch (e.g. `main`) can be checked out in **only one worktree at a time**.
- Kilo's agent manager creates a separate pooled worktree per session, usually detached at the base commit. `main` stays checked out in whichever worktree first claimed it (often the primary one, or a leftover pooled one).
- When you try to `git checkout main` in another worktree, Git refuses with `fatal: 'main' is already used by worktree at ...`.
- If you just `rm -rf` a pooled worktree directory instead of `git worktree remove`, the `.git/worktrees/<name>` registration can remain, so Git still thinks `main` is held there and the checkout keeps failing. Accumulating many pooled worktrees makes this worse.

What fixed it here: `git worktree remove <path>` (which clears the registration) followed by `git checkout main` once `main` was free. For cleanup of stale registrations you can also run `git worktree prune`.

The "can't commit to main" symptom comes from the same cause: commits made in a detached worktree create dangling commits that never advance `main`. To land work on `main`, either work in the worktree that actually has `main` checked out, or create a branch in the detached worktree and merge/fast-forward `main` afterward.